            <div class="step2-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Unique Contribution</h2>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/mandala-1.png">
                <p>The Caregiver Archetype provides structure to the world through their unique talent for caregiving.</p>
                <p>Caregivers bring their understanding of the importance of connecting with others and then apply structure to that notion through the act of caring and nurturing so that they can create what they see as ‘paradise.</p>
                <p>And in the eyes of The Caregiver Archetype, paradise is a place where the whole world is happy, healthy and loved unconditionally, including themselves.</p>
                <p><strong>The Unspoken Hero</strong></p>
                <p>Such dreams and ideals may seem admirable, even heroic to other archetypes who have different goals and ideas about what paradise is.</p>
                <p>In reality though, The Caregiver Archetype is often the unspoken hero, their efforts often pass by unnoticed and 
                unrewarded by many, but still, that doesn’t stop them.</p>
                <p>Whether the Caregiver consciously intends to help others, or not, they will be compelled towards and drawn into situations that involve caring for others.</p>
                <p>It’s how they make sense of the world even if they know that sometimes they sacrifice a little too much of their ‘self’ in the process! </p>
                <div class="prev-next">
                    <div class="container">
                        <div class="row mx-auto col-reverse">
                            <div class="col-sm-6 mx-auto">
                                <button class="btn step1-back hover prev f-left">1. Introduction</button>
                            </div>
                            <div class="col-sm-6">
                                <button class="btn step3 hover f-right">3. Sense of Significance</button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step3-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Sense of Significance</h2>
                <p>Attaining a sense of significance is a vital need for everybody.  If we don’t figure out a way to feel significant, we can start to become stagnant, we might develop poor confidence, and our wellbeing begins to deplete.  </p>
                <p>Most people naturally figure out a way to develop their sense of significance, and in the case of The Caregiver Archetype, they feel significant when they are helping others.  They do so by getting hands-on; at home, through their work, by providing information or by just checking in on somebody when times are tough for them.  </p>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/sun-glow.png" style="filter:none">
                <p><strong>Self-Sacrificial</strong></p>
                <p>Being friendly and sociable by nature The Caregiver Archetype has an innate radar for finding people who need help and then using their own ‘self’ to help the person in need, regardless of what it takes.</p>
                <p>This self-sacrificial tactic is the best way that a Caregiver will find to solve the problems that they see, and a person in need is a problem for the Caregiver. Even if some Caregivers don’t always consciously recognize that they place so much importance on helping others - they’ll still be helping people without realizing what they are doing. </p>
                <p><strong>Creative In Their Approach To Caring</strong></p>
                <p>To help people, some Caregivers might not look directly at the specific problem in hand. Instead, they might work in, or create a career that helps to solve a bigger problem - they might even work in charitable or social organizations.  </p>
                <p>But it wouldn’t be a surprise to find out that even this type of Caregiver also has a few people they are ‘watching out for’ in their everyday life too!</p>
                <p><strong>Caregiving is the Caregivers life’s work.  </strong></p>
                <p>As we move through this report, we will discover how many Caregivers bring problems to their door through their caring nature and NEED to help others.  We’ll even cover how the Caregiver can manage this problem in the future and also why the Caregiver should address these issues!</p>
                <p>The problems that a Caregiver brings to themselves because Caregiving is their life's work are some of the Caregivers biggest lessons, and for many, they also represent the Caregivers greatest challenge in life too.</p>
                <p><strong>Your Problem Is The Caregiver’s Problem</strong></p>
                <p>The Caregiver often provides practical help for others either through their work or by donating their spare time and resources to provide such care (or both).  </p>
                <p>They can and have been known to take on other people’s problems as if they are their own, even if the person who has the problem has not asked them to do that.</p>  
                <p><strong>Caregivers NEED To Save People</strong></p>
                <p>Perhaps Caregivers see themselves as ‘saviors,’ or maybe they have so much empathy and compassion that they cannot stand to see another person suffer, but whatever it is that drives them, there will be something that makes the Caregiver HAVE to help, and in many situations, they also NEED to help.  </p>
                <p>Maybe the Caregiver needs to justify their happiness (or even existence) through caring, perhaps they need to feel needed, or they might need to learn how to handle their deep compassion proactively without reacting to it all of the time. These ideas are something that a Caregiver would benefit from exploring, for sure.  </p>
                <p>Some Caregivers may have already come to realize the reasons why they might have such a need to ‘save’ people.  Others who have not discovered so much about themselves have the opportunity to learn more by working with their shadow self.  Which gives the Caregiver the chance to reflect and understand why they are driven to do what they do, and why they sacrifice their own needs to help others too.  </p>
                <div class="prev-next">
                    <div class="container">
                        <div class="row mx-auto col-reverse">
                            <div class="col-sm-6 mx-auto">
                                <button class="btn step2-back hover f-left">2. Unique Contribution</button>
                            </div>
                            <div class="col-sm-6">
                                <button class="btn step4 hover f-right">4. Personality Traits</button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step4-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Personality Traits</h2>
                <p><strong>Caregivers Help People Just Because They Can!</strong></p>
                <p>If you asked a Caregiver Archetype why they help others so much, there’s a high probability that they won’t be able to give you an answer other than that they help because people need help.  </p>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/floral.png">
                <p>Take a look at social sites such as Pinterest, and you’ll see plenty of pins that allude to quotes about practicing kindness and being loving and caring (probably all posted by Caregiver Archetypes) - they take pride in the notion of caring.  </p>
                <p>It’s something that is incredibly important for them.  </p>
                <p>Yet if you looked for quotes from a Hero Archetype for example, if they’ve taken the time to share any insight, it will all be about having courage, strength and facing their fears because that’s what is essential to The Hero.  </p>
                <p>The Heroes intentions and goals might confuse a Caregiver Archetype. The idea that people have other priorities that don’t involve helping others through acts of kindness and hands-on help will be alien to a Caregiver archetype. </p>
                <p>The Caregivers notion of courage will be entirely different to The Heroes. And regardless of how anybody perceives courage or any other quality, a Caregiver can’t see what would be more important than helping people.</p>
                <p>This desire to help is one of the Caregiver’s dominant personality traits.  </p>
                <p>You see, it’s not acceptable, or even possible for a Caregiver to easily walk away from somebody in need.  In the most extreme expression of The Caregiver Archetype, they cannot walk past somebody who needs help without helping them, or without feeling guilty because they can’t help them. </p>
                <p><strong>Sensitive Souls With Pure Hearts</strong></p>
                <p>Caregiver Archetypes can also be extremely sensitive to the plight of others; they can become overwhelmed and unbalanced if they allow themselves to consider the state of the world too much.  </p>
                <p>The amount of suffering that exists in the world is unbearably heartbreaking for a Caregiver not just because of the suffering though, but because there is nothing that they can do about it.  </p>
                <p><strong>The Hidden Personality Traits Of The Caregiver Archetype </strong></p>
                <p><strong>Why Do We Hide Ourselves Even from Ourselves?</strong></p>
                <p>Every person has a hidden side.  Jung calls it a shadow aspect, and it’s discussed in great detail in our Premium Archetype Reports.  </p>
                <p>Here at Individuologist.com we realize that there is a hidden side or an unconscious world (to be more precise) that we experience internally, but we don’t, or can't show or express this existence outwardly.</p>
                <p>It’s this hidden aspect that can bring about great reward if we were to focus our attention on it. It’s also crucial for helping each different archetype overcome their greatest challenge.  </p>
                <p>Before we get started, it’s important to acknowledge that there’s also a hidden part of us that remains unconscious even to ourselves too and the process of individuation can help us to uncover these hidden aspects of our ‘self’ too.  </p>
                <p>When we start to uncover these ‘hidden’ aspects of our ‘self,’ we begin to learn about what is really driving us. </p>
                <p>We discover the reasons why we do the things we do (or don’t do the things that we want to do, or should do).</p>
                <p>This gives us the opportunity to start to consciously reconcile these hidden aspects so that we can achieve our deepest desires, or reach our fullest potential.  
                <p>Even the Caregiver, who hands over most of themselves to others can start to put themselves first in a way that they feel comfortable by working on their shadow side.  Even if right now they can’t see how.</p>
                <p><strong>The Caregiver's Hidden Self</strong></p>
                <p>The Caregiver's hidden side is likely to be rooted in how they experience love and care (positively or negatively), why they have such a drive to help people and how they can find some equilibrium in their own experience of life. </p>
                <p>Most Caregivers, as we’ve already discussed, have an innate need to care, but it doesn’t mean that they are always comfortable with this situation, they can’t stop themselves. </p>
                <p><strong>Caregivers Don’t Always Want To Care - They Have To</strong></p>
                <p>Caregivers often sacrifice their own needs for the sake of others which can lead to feelings of frustration and lack in their own life. </p>
                <p>The irony is that they will likely repress or even feel guilty about having such feelings, since thinking about themselves when somebody in need is likely to seem like a selfish or ungrateful act to a Caregiver </p>
                <p>And Caregivers are far from selfish. Selfishness is not something they consciously identify with.</p>
                <p><strong>To Be Selfish, Or Not To Be Selfish?</strong></p>
                <p>Even though a Caregiver might unconsciously live in conflict with themselves, they frequently chastise themselves for being secretly ‘selfish’ (in their opinion) because they want some time out - or because they occasionally wish that they could just have a day or period where they don’t ‘have’ to care.</p>
                <p>The Caregiver Archetype doesn’t usually acknowledge that they too have a selfish part that is very real, valid and important.</p>
                <p>Selfishness is a controversial notion for a Caregiver since they usually dislike acts of selfishness so much.</p>
                <p>A Caregivers perspective on selfishness is likely to be that it’s not a good or admirable quality.</p>
                <p>However, we all need to have some element of selfishness to protect ourselves and to navigate our lives healthily.</p>
                <p>If there were a scale on selfishness of 1-10 (with one being the least selfish), a Caregiver would probably be at zero!  And may unconsciously strive for that number too!</p>
                <p>But for anybody to live healthily, a person needs to be balanced and at around a number 5 on the scale.  Otherwise, they don’t take care of themselves, nor do they protect themselves from other people’s undesirable and overly selfish traits. </p>
                <p>This unconscious conflict and natural desire for balance may be ignored, hidden or denied by a Caregiver Archetype because it doesn’t align with their self-sacrificial nature.  </p>
                <p><strong>No Is Not an Option</strong></p>
                <p>A Caregiver might also hide themselves in the way that they communicate since they often find it difficult to say no.</p>
                <p>Caregivers are not always the best people to ask for an honest perspective on something, especially when the truth involves having to relay some hard facts, most Caregivers can’t tell you the hard truth because they don’t want to ‘hurt your feelings.’</p>
                <p>Don’t ask a Caregiver if your questionable outfit looks good; they’ll probably say it does to save you from disappointment and will feel entirely justified in this little white lie!  </p>
                <p>A Caregiver will also reluctantly say ‘yes’ when they really mean ‘no,’ which makes it hard for others to figure out whether their Caregiver friend or family member wants to do something or not. </p>
                <p><strong>Falling Foul to Martyrdom </strong></p>
                <p>A Caregiver wants to leave the world better-off as a result of being in it and will sacrifice all of their dreams to help others because that is the way that they go about things.</p>
                <p>But they become drained and tired this way and don’t always give people the space to dig into their own resources to help themselves -  which can be counter-intuitive to their efforts for both parties involved. </p>
                <p><strong>A Deep Unfulfilled Need for Love</strong></p>
                <p>All of these thoughts and actions are often unconscious and hidden from the Caregiver, as is their constant and often unfulfilled need to be loved or feel loved.  </p>
                <p>The conflict they experience here is not often shown in public, or even admitted to themselves (for that would be selfish). Left unreconciled, in later life the Caregiver may become tired, drained, and bitter, even turning to martyrdom as a result. </p>
                <p>None of these thoughts are out of malice; it’s quite the opposite it’s always out of a misguided approach to making people feel good because the Caregiver wants everybody to feel good including themselves.</p>
                <p>The problem is though; nobody can love and fill up a Caregiver in the way that they want to feel loved and cared for.  And so, until the Caregiver starts to learn to apply self-love, they will always feel as though they’ve been stuck out at sea for far too long if they don’t watch themselves.</p>
